# Block Quest — Permanent Project Rules

Commercial 2D block puzzle. Portrait mobile (Android/iOS) + browser for development. Stack: TypeScript, Phaser 4, Vite, Vitest, Capacitor 8 (later).

## Before Any Task

1. Read `docs/PROJECT_OVERVIEW.md` before major work.
2. Read `docs/CURRENT_STATUS.md` before starting a task.
3. Read `docs/DEVELOPMENT_ROADMAP.md` to identify the current phase.
4. Read `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` before changing architecture.
5. Read `docs/DEVELOPMENT_WORKFLOW.md` before implementation.
6. Read `docs/DECISIONS.md` before changing an established technical decision.
7. Inspect the actual codebase — do not assume files or implementations exist.

Reconstruct context from the repository every chat. Never rely on prior chat memory alone.

## Coding Standards

- Make small, focused, reviewable changes. Avoid unnecessary refactoring.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies. Inspect `package.json` before installing. Do not upgrade without a technical reason.
- Use strict TypeScript.
- Keep business/game logic separate from Phaser rendering. Domain must not import Phaser.
- Write and maintain relevant tests. Never invent successful test results. Never claim something works without verification.
- Handle errors and edge cases. Never silently remove existing functionality.
- Follow existing codebase patterns. Update documentation when implementation or decisions change.
- No iOS native project on Windows. Do not add ads/analytics until approved phase.

## Hard Prohibitions

- NEVER push code to Git, publish the app, upload store builds, or purchase services.
- NEVER make destructive changes without explaining impact.
- Local development commands and tests are allowed when required.

## After Every Milestone

Update `docs/CURRENT_STATUS.md`, `docs/CHANGELOG.md`, `docs/DEVELOPMENT_ROADMAP.md`, and `docs/DECISIONS.md` when decisions change. Provide a completion report. Never mark a phase complete unless acceptance criteria are satisfied.

If docs and code disagree: inspect implementation, identify the gap, do not silently rewrite working code, then update docs appropriately.
